  {
    "episode_id": 71792,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71792.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 52,
    "turn_text": "Well, I grew up in a law enforcement family. My father was a manager of State Police radio stations. He was also the mayor of a little town in Goodell, Iowa, up in North Central Iowa, there in my grade school years. That made him the justice of the peace, the magistrate, you might say. And so he sat in on cases like running a stop sign and those kind of things. But the town cop one day, and I guess I would have been in about fourth or fifth grade, Henry Noldie, the town cop, arrested an illegal Mexican, and they didn't know what to do with him, so they brought him before my dad's little magistrate court. And I remember him coming back home that day and telling the story, and he said, what do you do? Well, he's here illegally, you need to go back home. So I just sentenced him to deportation, and we called up the Highway Patrol, who he was a dispatcher of them anyway, and said, put that guy in your car driving towards the state line and shuttling back to Mexico. Now, I don't know if he ever made it back to Mexico or not, but I got the message, which is when somebody's in violation of the law, the least you can do is put them back in the condition they were in before they broke it. And so that was a pretty good lesson. But then also I grew up at Denison, which is one of the more today, we'd say one of the more multicultural counties in the country. heavy meatpacking, and we had the first wave of Vietnamese in 1975. Most of them migrated towards the largest city over time. They were replaced then by folks from southern Mexico, and as that flooded in, my hometown that I was born in was storm-like. Those towns are an hour apart. I live halfway in between them. Both of them are heavy packing plant towns and both of them, the population has been well overcome by Central Americans. And at least that's now what Somali is coming in. But just watching that and being part of it and seeing what it's done from the crime standpoint for one thing. And now I walk into the elementary school. Last time I counted 85% of the kids there were challenged with English, and they came from non-English speaking homes. So I live in the middle of it, and even though it's the middle of America, I live in the middle of it. And I will say, Scott, I understand it. I've gone to the border from San Diego to Brownsville, I've set foot on many of the miles along the way and I've flown a lot of it, driven a lot of it. I've slept down there on the border. I've collared some of them. I've unloaded illegal drugs. I've been into their courtroom. I pay attention to this because there's something about immigration distinct from every other policy and that is you can't underdo a mistake on immigration. It multiplies itself over the generations. So we better get it right the first time.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
